const abstractText = "Serverless (on-demand, scale-to-zero) deployment trades idle-resource cost for cold-start latency whenever traffic resumes after a period of silence -- a pattern that recurs constantly in real-time edge web applications. Predictive pre-warming -- forecasting the next burst and provisioning capacity ahead of it -- is a natural mitigation. Cold-start mitigation research spans a spectrum from simple, deployed heuristics to heavy learned forecasters: at the lightweight end, Shahrad et al. [4] describe a hybrid histogram-and-ARIMA keep-alive/pre-warming policy already deployed at a large commercial FaaS provider; at the heavy end, recent work reports recurrent neural networks and model-predictive-control forecasters with substantial reported gains. This paper asks a narrower question: within the near-zero-cost forecaster family itself, how much of the achievable benefit is reachable without any adaptive learning at all, and through what mechanism? We built an open, reproducible harness comparing an always-on container deployment, a purely reactive on-demand deployment, and four lightweight predictive policies -- an exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA), a naive last-observed-gap forecaster, a simple moving average, and a fixed-schedule forecaster assuming a constant period -- under three workload regimes, with independently reset trials, seeds matched across policies, and trial-level non-parametric statistical testing. This design, and two bugs it caught in an earlier draft (a self-referential forecasting error and a prewarm-trigger timing-reference mismatch), are disclosed in full in the published changelog as part of the reproducibility record. Under repeated idle-then-burst cyclical traffic (15 independent trials per policy, 6,750 requests), a latency decomposition shows the dominant cost is not cold-start duration itself (mean 75-119 ms) but time spent queueing for capacity once a burst exceeds the instance-count limit (mean 171-481 ms, affecting 80% of requests) -- a capacity-bound bottleneck that is partly a structural consequence of this study's specific burst-size-to-concurrency-cap ratio (15 requests against 3 instances) and partly genuinely measured; a single pre-warmed instance can only partially relieve it. Under a paired, repeated-measures analysis (Friedman omnibus across all six policies, chi-squared=56.49, df=5, p=6.5e-11, followed by two separately Holm-corrected planned-comparison families), all four lightweight predictive policies achieve a statistically significant latency reduction relative to the purely reactive baseline (Holm-adjusted p=0.036 EWMA, p=0.020 last-gap, p=0.036 moving-average, p=0.0002 fixed-schedule; matched-pairs rank-biserial effect sizes -0.62 to -1.00). Among the four, the three adaptive forecasters are statistically indistinguishable from one another; fixed-schedule significantly outperforms all three (Holm-adjusted p~=0.0004 each) -- an advantage that must be read together with two caveats: its constant period was set with knowledge of the workload generator's own periodicity, not learned, and a sensitivity-sweep pilot (N=6/condition) shows this advantage is fragile, unaffected by under-estimating the true period by up to 50% but largely erased by a 10% over-estimate, and inverted (fixed-schedule becomes slightly worse than plain reactive scaling) at a 50% over-estimate. A second pilot tests a non-oracle keep-alive baseline (idle timeout swept over 8/10/12 s against a 6 s reactive default): it shows no benefit at 8 s (the workload's own disclosed minimum idle gap) but a sharp step-function improvement at or above the workload's own disclosed maximum gap (10 s), where cold-start rate falls from 20% to 4%. In a dedicated 25-cycle persistent-state run, EWMA's observed persistent-state behavior reduces user-facing cold starts from 3 to 2 per cycle (one of three anticipated ahead of time), stable over the 25 cycles, once repeated trial-reset warm-up is removed. Under the generalized two-resource cost model, EWMA and moving-average are net CPU-second savers relative to reactive at a small GB-second premium; last-gap and fixed-schedule cost more in both. All code, raw per-request data, the reanalysis and pilot-experiment scripts, and Kubernetes/Knative manifests for future real-platform validation are openly published at " + GITHUB_URL + ".";

const kw = "Serverless computing; cold start; predictive autoscaling; container orchestration; edge computing; queueing; reproducible research; cost-latency trade-off";

